[thin] These are the tongue's 1.68 mm thick bedded wings, with 2.40 mm reach beyond the 18.00 mm body. All five functional tongue geometries retain the same wing thickness and reach while their receiver X air changes. The wings are supported directly by the bed; their thickness is part of the flat-wing vocabulary used in the full grip, not a general structural wall exception.
alternatives: A 3.00 mm wing can use a taller channel while retaining 0.45 mm roof air, but changes the specimen's wing section, stiffness and total vertical stack. Reducing reach leaves a larger root-to-tip section only by giving up capture area. Replacing the wing slide with a solid rectangular tongue eliminates these thin wings but no longer represents this retention mechanism.
